    "abstract": "The main sources of greenhouse gas emissions are fossil fuels, with electricity generation and road transportation contributing most significantly. Enhancing route planning can reduce traffic-related energy consumption and associated emissions. Although widely used route planning maps (e.g., Google Maps) offer shortest, fastest, or eco-friendly routes, they often omit high-impact parameters influencing power demand, including road topology, engine performance, vehicle dimensions, resistance factors, and driving behaviour, limiting their effectiveness for the most energy-efficient routing.This paper presents a novel parameterized road vehicle energy consumption model based on the state of the art, expanding on the authors’ previous work by incorporating additional parameters to provide a model that balances a highly accurate estimate of energy consumption with a simple parameter configuration, powertrain calibration and low calculation/computation overhead. The proposed model’s estimations closely match real vehicle onboard computer measurements across diverse vehicle engine types and routes, demonstrating its accuracy.",
